WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.560
In the previous lecture, we have discussed&nbsp;
that machine learning algorithms can be broadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.320
divided into 3 categories: supervised learning,&nbsp;
unsupervised learning, and reinforcement learning.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.400
In supervised learning we give the computer a&nbsp;
data set with what is called a target variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.360
That's whether or not the person had heart&nbsp;
disease, or did not have heart disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.920
We then give a series of input variables. The&nbsp;
modeling algorithms automatically generates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.640
the model, which establishes a relationship&nbsp;
between the target and the input variables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.360
In other words, you give the inputs,&nbsp;
and along with the input, you give the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.800
outcome.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.280
Unsupervised learning is a search for&nbsp;
homogeneous subsets that produces one or more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.000
possible segmentations of the data that differ&nbsp;
by how similar the cases are within the segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
and how many subsets there are, and how common&nbsp;
or rare they are. The whole idea of homogeneous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.120
is just put similar cases together. Now,&nbsp;
the computer will help you do the search.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.680
But, you have to decide how similar is&nbsp;
similar enough for your application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.320
You have to decide how many subsets you want there&nbsp;
to be. And you have to decide how rare or common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.720
the subsets are going to be.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.920
Unsupervised is actually more difficult for the&nbsp;
human analyst than supervised in many cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.800
For instance, take a look. Let's just say we&nbsp;
were asking our computer to perform unsupervised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.920
segmentation on these blocks. Should we&nbsp;
separate them so that they're blue and non-blue?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.160
Or maybe red and non-red? Should we be&nbsp;
focusing on shape or is that less important?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.960
Should we be focusing on size? It's probably&nbsp;
impossible to answer outside some context.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.720
So, the computer will help group, but only in&nbsp;
partnership with a human analyst that knows what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.440
the goal of the segmentation will be.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.920
So sometimes, we do see definitions of&nbsp;
unsupervised learning that make it seem like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.640
it's pretty much supervised, but when you can't&nbsp;
find a target variable or you don't have ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.640
access to a target variable. It's actually a very&nbsp;
different set of goals when we do unsupervised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.920
learning.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.360
The third area of machine learning&nbsp;
is called reinforcement learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.360
Only recently has this been a source of confusion,&nbsp;
mostly because of two application areas that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.880
gotten a lot of excitement. Technologies like&nbsp;
driverless cars and technologies like the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.880
that was able to beat the world champion at go,&nbsp;
these operate in a very different way. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.720
they sound like supervised. But they're quite&nbsp;
different. What's happening here is you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:47.120
presenting the algorithm with a very large number&nbsp;
of input and output pairs. Rather, it's working in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.240
an environment and it's correct and incorrect not&nbsp;
for each row of data, but it's rather rewarded for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.840
correct actions it takes in the environment. This&nbsp;
is the kind of technology that arises for very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.000
cool things like watching a robot stumble&nbsp;
and then as it practices it gets better.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.960
Next, let's try and understand&nbsp;
unsupervised machine learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.600
Put simply, this is the process of&nbsp;
taking a lot of unstructured data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.520
running it through lots of compute, and&nbsp;
somehow getting structured data out of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.400
Example, let's say that in a room there&nbsp;
are five people talking with each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:27.040
That's a lot of unstructured data, but everybody's&nbsp;
voice is like a thumbprint. So just by having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.720
computer analyze it we could split that into&nbsp;
individual audio streams. Or, let's say we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.320
have lots of documents, and we wish to be able&nbsp;
to categorize them. But, we don't know how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.520
categories, but we want to categorize them.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.840
Or, you got lots of pictures, and you want&nbsp;
to find similarities between those pictures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.880
This is not just recognizing faces, so let's&nbsp;
say person X appears in these pictures. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.520
a very common application, but it could also&nbsp;
be how many pictures are daytime or nighttime?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.160
How many pictures have bright lights in them? How&nbsp;
many pictures have modern buildings versus nature?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:11.280
These are various categories. So, these are&nbsp;
various examples of unsupervised machine learning.&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.400
&nbsp;
At a high level you can think of unsupervised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.680
learning as something that where data points have&nbsp;
no labels associated with them. So you don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.520
what categories these will fall into. The goal is&nbsp;
to somehow organize the data to make sense of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.960
The important point here is that unsupervised&nbsp;
machine learning can often be a precursor to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.280
supervised machine learning.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:39.280
Example that we have five people talking, and we&nbsp;
get individual audio streams out of them. Now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.160
you have split those audio streams, perhaps,&nbsp;
we can try, and find out what language they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:50.960
talking in? What is the sentiment? We can analyze&nbsp;
the text, try and gain meaning, or knowledge out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.840
of what they were talking about. All of that&nbsp;
then gets into supervised machine learning.

